<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Netilas Yadayim L'Seudah — Betzi'as HaPas<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">(Part 3: §§ 32–43)</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam Yenahaigem"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM II, 7</span>)</span> — continuation.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 32 — Barley to Wheat: Rectifying Adam's Sin Through Eating</div>

<div class="p">Further explanation is needed here. For initially one must rectify the aspect of <span class="kc">animal food</span> — the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley. And afterward, on <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, one merits the <span class="kc">two loaves of wheat</span> — human food. This is the rectification of the sin of Adam HaRishon, to whom it was first said: <span class="v">"you shall eat the grass of the field"</span> — animal food, as our Sages say: he trembled and said, <span class="v">"I and the donkey at one trough?"</span> And afterward [Hashem] said to him: <span class="v">"By the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of eating <span class="h">karpas</span> on Pesach before the Seder begins — a type of vegetable — and afterward eating <span class="h">matzah</span>. For the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> is to merit the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating, drawn from the aspect of silence — <span class="mx">"one does not converse during a meal."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 33 — The Wondrous Unity of the Torah Teaching</div>

<div class="p">For the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Ki Merachamam"</span> (and indeed all the Torah teachings) is <span class="kc">bound together in a wondrous knot</span>. For at first it wrote that one must illuminate the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> in every person — this is the essential compassion — to extract him from sins that come through <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>. Then it revealed further that the <span class="h">tzadik</span> illuminates in the upper and lower dwellers — informing those lying in the lowest depths, in the lowest pit of <span class="h">She'ol</span> and below it, that even there Hashem is still with them, for <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — and awakening them in the aspect of <span class="v">"awaken and sing, dwellers of the dust."</span></div>

<div class="p">In truth, it is all one matter. For through this itself — that [the <span class="h">tzadik</span>] has the power to inform the lower ones, even the exceedingly fallen, that Hashem is still with them — through this itself he illuminates in them the <span class="h">da'as</span> and drives away the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> each time. For it is certainly a great labor and exceedingly wondrous craft to illuminate <span class="h">da'as</span> in such people who have stumbled in sins — especially those who stumbled deliberately in tremendous sins, may the Merciful One save us. Yet the <span class="h">tzadik</span> is such a <span class="kc">compassionate one</span> that even in them he illuminates the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> through his goodness and mercy. For through his great power, he informs them that <span class="kc">Hashem is still with them</span>, that <span class="mx">"there is no place empty of Him."</span> Through this itself, the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> is driven away each time.</div>

<div class="p">For even if a person is however he is — the moment he is informed that Hashem is found even there, as it is written: <span class="v">"Who dwells with them in the midst of their impurity"</span> <span class="sr">(Leviticus 16:16)</span> — for even in the ten crowns of impurity Hashem is found, since all their vitality is from Him alone — immediately the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> flees, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"the idols of Egypt tremble before Him"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 19:1)</span>, and: <span class="v">"I shall pass through the land of Egypt — and I shall be revealed… I am Hashem, and not an angel"</span> — and then: <span class="v">"I shall smite every firstborn"</span> — which is the <span class="h">da'as</span> of the <span class="h">sitra achra</span>, from which the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> [derives]. For who would dare approach Him?</div>

<div class="p">All of this is the aspect of offering the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley — animal food, the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> — that the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe, with his great power can elevate even the barley, animal food, <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> — sifting it through thirteen sieves until he refines even from there holy and wondrous sparks, until everything ascends as a pleasing fragrance to Hashem. For within the animalistic itself, within the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> itself, His G-dly vitality is hidden — sustaining them all. Therefore, through waving the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley and offering it to Hashem, one reveals that even in all the lowest levels where all the stragglers and fallen have descended — <span class="kc">even there Hashem is found</span>, for <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore we immediately begin counting the days for the <span class="h">Omer</span>. For every day contains wisdom and <span class="h">da'as</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"days shall speak"</span> <span class="sr">(Job 32:7)</span>. Now, through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of counting, we bind each day to the <span class="h">Omer</span> — to exit thereby from the contamination of Egypt. For by binding the days to the <span class="h">Omer</span>, we reveal that even within the contamination of Egypt itself, within the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> itself, Hashem is hidden. Through this itself, the contamination — the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> — flees. For through the counting, we bind time and the days to the aspect of above time — <span class="h">tekufos hayamim</span> — which are the secret of the <span class="h">makifin</span> drawn through the counting. This is the inclusiveness of the lower in the upper — for there, in the aspect of the upper, it is above time. Time exists only in the aspect of the lower, where there is counting and number. For <span class="mx">"before the One, what do you count?"</span> — but we, the children of Israel, through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation who are the aspect of Moshe, bind through the counting the days and time to the aspect of above time — the inclusiveness of the lower in the upper, the essential completeness — <span class="v">"<span class="h">temimos</span> shall they be."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 34 — The Seven Times Seven: Illuminating Every <i>Midah</i></div>

<div class="p">We count seven weeks — forty-nine days, seven times seven — which are the inclusiveness of all the <span class="h">midos</span> [attributes], to illuminate in every single <span class="h">midah</span>. They are the seven days of building. However a person blemished in each <span class="h">midah</span> according to his deeds, even from there he will be aroused to Hashem each day through the power of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe, who knows the secret of waving the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley — animal food. Through this, each day one draws upon himself some illumination of the <span class="h">Ratzon</span> — until on <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, one merits to draw the <span class="kc"><span class="h">Keser</span></span> (which is the entire intention of <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, as explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>) — the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>, the flow of the <span class="h">Keser</span>, drawn specifically during eating. This is the <span class="kc">two loaves of wheat</span> — human food — which equals <span class="h">mah</span> [45], the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> = <span class="h">mah</span>, the aspect of <span class="v">"at the time it shall be said… <span class="h">mah</span>…"</span></div>

<div class="p">For during all the days of the counting, one rectified the aspect of animal food through the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley — illuminating and revealing that even within animal food, one can draw and illuminate the truth of His G-dliness, knowing that Hashem is found even there, meriting even then the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating. This means: even the simple person, whose eating is not yet at the level of human food in completeness, should also be a man of valor and not be lazy — he should strive during his eating to draw upon himself the wondrous illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span>. For <span class="kc">every person can merit this</span> through the power of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> — specifically at the time of eating, he should draw upon himself the <span class="h">yirah</span> that comes to a person then, until he merits the wondrous illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> specifically during eating. And each time, as the illumination of <span class="h">yirah</span> and <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is drawn upon him, his eating is proportionally refined from animal food to human food — until he merits human food in completeness.</div>

<div class="p">This is the <span class="h">Omer</span> counting and <span class="h">Shavuos</span>: initially the <span class="h">Omer</span> of barley, animal food; then on <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, the two loaves, human food. For <span class="h">Ratzon</span> is always free — it is the aspect of the great <span class="h">Yovel</span> [Jubilee], the fiftieth gate, where every person goes out to freedom, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"Fortunate is the land whose king is a <span class="h">ben chorin</span> [free man], and whose ministers eat in due time"</span> <span class="sr">(Ecclesiastes 10:17)</span> — as explained in the Torah teaching, see there well to connect it to all the above. Meaning: even in his bitter and exceedingly bitter exile, having strayed into such <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> — whether in his thoughts and terrible ruminations, or in the matter of livelihood where he fell from holy <span class="h">bitachon</span> into vain and empty trusts — the aspect of <span class="v">"the people went out and gathered"</span> — <span class="h">b'shtusa</span> — <span class="kc">even from there he should draw upon himself the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span></span>, drawn at its root from the world of freedom, from <span class="h">Yovel</span>, from the fiftieth gate. For despite everything, I desire with a strong desire toward Hashem — for the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> have already informed us that Hashem is still with me and near me and close to me always, for <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory."</span></div>

<div class="p">Through this, one draws upon himself each time the <span class="h">da'as</span> and <span class="h">Ratzon</span> even in the aspect of animal food — until animal food, the barley, is refined and one merits human food. This is the two loaves of <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, when the flow of the <span class="h">Keser</span> is drawn upon all Israel — from which the essential illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> [derives]. There is the essential purification and <span class="h">tikkun</span> from all sins — the aspect of the <span class="h">mikveh</span> of <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, the <span class="h">mikveh</span> of the fiftieth gate, the aspect of the <span class="h">Keser</span>. All of this is <span class="v">"this great and wide sea"</span> — the <span class="h">remazim</span>, <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, the <span class="h">mikveh</span> of <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> during eating — the two loaves of wheat brought on <span class="h">Shavuos</span>, for which this entire holy and awesome festival is named, as it is written: <span class="v">"On the day of the first-fruits, when you bring a new meal-offering to Hashem"</span> <span class="sr">(Numbers 28:26)</span>. For this is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>: to merit human food, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> = <span class="h">mah</span>, during eating.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 35 — Adam Trembled — and Through That Trembling, Was Healed</div>

<div class="p">All of this rectifies the sin of Adam HaRishon, whose entire blemish was in eating. Now we rectify it: first in the aspect of <span class="v">"you shall eat the grass of the field"</span> — animal food — for even animal food will be refined by drawing <span class="h">da'as</span> and the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> even there. Afterward one merits human food — <span class="v">"by the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is hinted in the words of our Sages: when Hashem said to Adam <span class="v">"you shall eat the grass of the field,"</span> he trembled and said, <span class="v">"I and the donkey at one trough?"</span> When Hashem said <span class="v">"by the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread,"</span> his mind was settled. Meaning: through the very fact that he <span class="kc">trembled</span> when Hashem told him his eating would be animal food — through this itself he merited Hashem's promise of human food. For even when one's eating is still at the level of animal food, he must draw upon himself the <span class="h">yirah</span> that comes during eating — until he yearns with a strong <span class="h">Ratzon</span> to merit human food. And then, through this itself, he merits it. Just as Adam HaRishon: when told <span class="v">"you shall eat the grass of the field"</span> — he trembled. <span class="kc">Trembled, specifically</span> — he drew upon himself <span class="h">yirah</span>. And he cried out: <span class="v">"I and the donkey at one trough?"</span> — meaning: I want and plead that You take me out of the aspect of animal food. And immediately Hashem was appeased and said: <span class="v">"By the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread"</span> — through the sweat of your brow, meaning exertions in Torah and service, which is the aspect of <span class="v">"the scent of your breath like apples"</span> <span class="sr">(Song of Songs 7:9)</span>, the exertions to draw close to the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who draws one close to Hashem (as explained in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Tik'u Emunah"</span>, §16) — through this, <span class="v">"you shall eat bread"</span>: you will merit human food, the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> in ultimate completeness.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 36 — The 320 Sparks and the Word <i>Adam</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is what is explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span> of the counting: that each day one must intend to refine the <span class="kc">320 (<span class="h">sha"ch</span>) sparks</span> — to draw <span class="h">mah</span> (45) sparks each day that equal <span class="h">Adam</span> — until in the seven days of the week the 320 sparks are completed (seven times <span class="h">Adam</span> [45] = 315, plus the <span class="h">Hei</span> of the Name). For it is known that all of man's service is to refine the holy sparks that fell among the <span class="h">klipos</span> and <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> through his sins. The essential blemish is that one fell from <span class="h">da'as</span> — from the aspect of <span class="h">Adam</span> [man] to the aspect of <span class="h">beheimah</span> [animal], to the aspect of <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Through the <span class="h">tikkun</span> of the counting, we merit to purify ourselves from our <span class="h">klipos</span> and impurities, from all the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> of sin. The main thing: through the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, one knows and intends that even there within the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> itself, Hashem is hidden — there are sparks of His G-dliness sustaining them. This is the essential blemish of sin: it brought down such holy sparks to such filthy places. And this is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>: when one knows and believes there are holy sparks there — the vitality of His G-dliness — and he cleaves to Hashem even from there. Until he is aroused and returns to Hashem from wherever he is, gathering and refining sparks each day that equal <span class="h">Adam</span> — <span class="kc">transforming <span class="h">shatu</span></span> [they went out in folly] <span class="kc">into <span class="h">Adam</span></span> [man = <span class="h">da'as</span> = <span class="h">Ratzon</span> = <span class="h">mah</span>].</div>

<div class="p">All through the <span class="h">da'as</span> of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who illuminates in each person's heart that Hashem is still with him. And the main thing: through the <span class="h">remazim</span>. For this <span class="h">da'as</span> cannot be explained by mouth in completeness — only through <span class="h">remazim</span>, the aspect of hands in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, from which sustenance [flows]. For the <span class="h">tzadik</span> illuminates with his hands and <span class="h">remazim</span> to every single spark to awaken and arise from its great fall. For the <span class="h">remazim</span> are themselves the aspect of sparks — like <span class="kc">light flickering from afar through holes and cracks in the partition walls</span>. Therefore the sparks — the sparks of <span class="h">da'as</span> hidden within the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> — are aroused through the <span class="h">remazim</span> of the <span class="h">tzadik</span> that flash toward them, so they remember Hashem and return to Him — until they are gathered and become the aspect of <span class="h">Adam</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc">Understand this very, very well, for practical guidance and good counsel</span> — so that you do not deceive yourself, but understand from afar, through the <span class="h">remazim</span>, counsel at every turn: <span class="kc">never to despair of yourself in any way</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 37 — Even Madness Has Sparks of <i>Da'as</i></div>

<div class="p">For we see with our senses: there are people in whom one observes many types of actual madness — literal <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>. Yet they are not completely insane. This is because of the <span class="kc">sparks of <span class="h">da'as</span></span> remaining in them, through which they understand not to lose their minds entirely. But when one blemishes his <span class="h">da'as</span> greatly and follows the madness and <span class="h">ruach shtus</span>, he becomes literally insane.</div>

<div class="p">And it is known that every person, according to his sins, is "insane" — for a sinner is insane, as explained at the beginning of <span class="h">Likutay Mohara"n</span>: <span class="mx">"a person does not sin unless a <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> enters him."</span> According to the many sins found in many people, as is known, they should all become literally insane. But Hashem has mercy on His creatures and sustains their <span class="h">da'as</span> so they do not lose their minds entirely — through the aspect mentioned above: a residual imprint (<span class="h">reshimah</span>) remains in their <span class="h">da'as</span>, drawn from the <span class="h">remazim</span> and sparks.</div>

<div class="p">For whoever has stumbled and fallen into <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> still has free choice. As our <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>, of blessed memory, said to a certain person — he himself told him that he had a madness — and afterward said: <span class="kc">"If you want, you are not insane."</span> Understand this well. For even a literal madman, when struck properly, is forced to cast aside the madness out of fear. From all this, a person who truly wants to have pity on himself can overcome — gradually, with the little <span class="h">da'as</span> remaining to him — the <span class="h">ruach shtus</span> and madness that assails him each time. All through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadik</span> who hints to him <span class="h">remazim</span> and illuminates in him the <span class="h">da'as</span> — explicitly and through hint — with deep, good, and wholesome counsels. Fortunate is he who grasps this.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 38 — The <i>Makifin</i> Are Drawn Through Speech</div>

<div class="p">And this is what is explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>: the <span class="h">makifin</span> drawn during the counting are drawn primarily through <span class="kc">speech</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">gevuros</span>. For the essential inclusiveness is through the <span class="kc">mouth's speech</span>, through which one reveals the holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, in the aspect of <span class="v">"I will make Your faithfulness known with my mouth"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 89:2)</span>. For the inclusiveness mentioned above cannot be grasped by intellect — only one must strengthen in <span class="h">emunah</span> through the flashes the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> show us. Through this, one strengthens the holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, believing with complete faith as if seeing with our eyes that Hashem is with us, that this world is included in the upper and the upper in the lower — all joined and bound together, conducted by His simple will, in the aspect of <span class="v">"for all in heaven and earth."</span></div>

<div class="p">All this holy <span class="h">emunah</span> must be <span class="kc">spoken aloud</span>. This is what we draw during the counting — rectifying the speech through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of counting, as understood from the <span class="h">Kavanos</span>.</div>

<div class="p">And we engage in all of this in the order of daily prayer — <span class="h">Pesukei D'Zimra</span>, <span class="h">Krias Shema</span> and its <span class="h">berachos</span>, and the <span class="h">Amidah</span> — speaking with our mouths the holy <span class="h">emunah</span> that Hashem created everything. Through this, we include the upper in the lower and the lower in the upper: the attribute of night in day and day in night — for we must include day and night together, which are sun and moon, the luminous and non-luminous looking-glasses, the illumination of the upper and lower, as explained in the Torah teaching. This is the counting: <span class="v">"you shall count for yourselves… seven complete weeks"</span> — counting the days so each day should be <span class="h">tamim</span> [whole] by including day and night together. This is the preparation for receiving the Torah, whose essence is for this — the aspect of <span class="v">"know today and take it to your heart that Hashem — He is the G-d, in the heavens above"</span> [= the upper] <span class="v">"and upon the earth below"</span> [= the lower] <span class="v">"— there is none other"</span> <span class="sr">(Deuteronomy 4:39)</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we recite <span class="h">Kedushah</span> in the <span class="h">berachos</span> of <span class="h">Krias Shema</span> before <span class="h">Shema</span> itself: <span class="v">"The whole earth is full of His glory"</span> and <span class="v">"Blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place"</span> — the illumination of the lower (<span class="h">Melo</span>) and the illumination of the upper (<span class="h">Ayeh</span>). For the essential <span class="h">Krias Shema</span> — the <span class="h">emunah</span> of unity — is the inclusiveness of <span class="h">Ayeh</span> and <span class="h">Melo</span>: knowing and declaring with a full mouth that the singular, primordial Creator, hidden and concealed in the ultimate concealment — <span class="mx">"no thought grasps Him at all"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">mah</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Ayeh</span> — He created and formed and made everything, and <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span>, for He gives life to all.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 39 — <i>Krias Yam Suf</i>: The Essential Revelation of <i>Da'as</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the <span class="kc">splitting of the sea</span>. For then was the essential revelation of <span class="h">da'as</span>, as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"A maidservant at the sea saw what Yechezkel the prophet did not see"</span> — how much more so Israel, even the simple ones, how much more so the great ones — as explained (<span class="lr">LM II, 39</span>). The illumination of the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> of the inclusiveness of son and student — the inclusiveness of all the worlds — <span class="v">"this great and wide sea"</span> where the illumination of <span class="h">Ratzon</span> shines — was drawn upon them.</div>

<div class="p">For it is impossible to enter the physical sea due to the abundance of water. Similarly in the spiritual sea — <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>, root of the physical sea — it is impossible to enter due to the abundance of the waters of <span class="h">da'as</span>. Whoever tries to enter without a true <span class="h">Rebbe</span> and leader drowns in it without support — like Pharaoh and Egypt. One can only enter through true, wise <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who are the aspect of <span class="v">"those who go down to the sea in ships, who do business in great waters"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 107:23)</span>. Just as in the physical sea one can only cross through ships built with wisdom and great skill, with a captain who knows how to steer according to the hour and the winds — so too in the spiritual <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>: one needs a true <span class="h">tzadik</span>-leader who knows the pathways of the sea, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"Who makes a road in the sea and a path in mighty waters"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 43:16)</span>. And: <span class="v">"She is like the merchant ships; from afar she brings her bread"</span> <span class="sr">(Proverbs 31:14)</span> — for from there sustenance and bread are drawn, from the hands that are <span class="h">remazim</span> in <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">For the ultimate completeness of the wisdom cannot be expressed except through <span class="h">remazim</span> — for one cannot teach how to steer the ropes and mast and ship at every moment; one must understand on one's own according to the shifting winds — until sometimes no wisdom avails, only crying out to Hashem: <span class="v">"He spoke and a stormy wind arose… and all their wisdom was swallowed up… and they cried out to Hashem"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 107:25-28)</span>. How much more so in the spiritual <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential difficulty of <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> was because then was the completion of the redemption. And the completion of the redemption is the joining and inclusiveness of the worlds — the <span class="h">da'as</span> Moshe revealed at the Exodus. But it was not yet in complete perfection, since at the Exodus the <span class="h">da'as</span> was revealed without <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>. The true inclusiveness in completeness requires <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span> — for this is why Hashem created the world in such materiality, desiring our service specifically. And the main thing: because man has free choice, and the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span> and <span class="h">sitra achra</span> overpower every day — and when we arouse even slightly from below, this is more precious in His eyes than all the service of the angels.</div>

<div class="p">This was the entire difficulty of <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span>: Hashem had to silence the great accusation — <span class="mx">"these are idolaters and these are idolaters"</span> — by finding in His kindness some merits and good points in Israel that were aroused on their own. Through the <span class="h">da'as</span> Moshe had drawn upon them at the Exodus, they were aroused slightly on their own to His service — until they merited the aspect of <span class="kc">silence</span>: not to question His ways — the aspect of <span class="v">"Hashem shall fight for you, and you shall be silent"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 14:14)</span>. Through this, <span class="h">yam hachochmah</span> illuminated upon them properly, all the springs of wisdom burst open until <span class="v">"a maidservant at the sea saw…"</span> — until the sea split physically and they crossed on dry land. For they merited holy contractions and partitions through the silence — <span class="mx">"a fence for wisdom is silence."</span> But Pharaoh and Egypt — external wisdoms and intellects — pursued them and were drowned in the sea physically and spiritually. For whoever does not receive from his <span class="h">Rebbe</span>, the aspect of Moshe, the silence — the partitions of intellect, not to enter into investigations and <span class="h">kushyos</span> but to rely on <span class="h">emunah</span> — is drowned in the sea.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 40 — The Secret of the Serpent's Bite</div>

<div class="p">And this is the hidden secret explained in the <span class="h">Kavanos</span> of the seventh of Pesach: <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> was through the secret of <span class="mx">"that serpent that bites."</span> The essence of <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> was that Moshe Rabbainu, with his exceedingly great power, drew the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> of son and student down to the very, very lowest levels — informing even those lying in the lowest pit that they too should know Hashem is still with them, for <span class="v">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> — until even they are aroused with some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, believing in Hashem and in Moshe His servant. This is Hashem's essential delight: just as He sets aside all the upper ones — angels and seraphim — and desires specifically the service of physical humans, so too the lower and more fallen the level, when they are aroused with any arousal whatsoever, <span class="kc">wondrous delight is made above</span>, in the aspect of: <span class="mx">"when Yisro came, the Holy Name was honored and elevated."</span></div>

<div class="p">As brought in <span class="h">Rabbainu</span>'s words (<span class="lr">LM I, 13</span>) from the Holy Zohar — the concept of <span class="mx">"this creature, when it conceives…"</span> — it is understood there that specifically when the great <span class="h">tzadik</span> raises the exceedingly blemished souls — the embittered souls, the starving souls — and includes them among the other holy souls, raising them in the aspect of <span class="h">ibur</span> [gestation] — through this specifically the wondrous <span class="h">yichud</span> [unification] is made. This itself is the secret of <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span>: through the very souls in <span class="h">mochin d'katnus</span>, where the aspect of the serpent has great hold — through them specifically, through the power of the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Moshe of great stature — <span class="v">"What are you crying out to Me?"</span> — <span class="mx">"the matter depends on <span class="h">Atika</span>"</span> — for it is impossible to raise and rectify them except through <span class="h">Atika</span>, through <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika Stima'ah</span> [the Torah of the hidden Ancient One] — as explained in the Torah teaching <span class="v">"Mishrah"</span> and elsewhere: those most ill in sickness of the soul can only be helped by a very great <span class="h">Rebbe</span> who can illuminate in them from the exceedingly exalted level of <span class="h">Oraisa d'Atika Stima'ah</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 41 — Yosef's Bones and the Merit of the Avos</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> was through <span class="kc">Yosef's bones</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"The sea saw and fled"</span> <span class="sr">(Psalms 114:3)</span>. And through the <span class="kc">merit of the <span class="h">Avos</span></span>, as our Sages say. Yet it was difficult all night until in the morning He remembered their ancestors' merit. This is puzzling: could He not have remembered the merit of the <span class="h">Avos</span> earlier? But according to the above: the essential merit of the <span class="h">Avos</span> — Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov, Yosef, and all the seven shepherds — is that they all engaged in drawing down the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> to their children and children's children forever, to inform them that <span class="v">"Hashem — He is the G-d."</span></div>

<div class="p">But the essential completeness of this holy path is that <span class="kc">we, their descendants</span>, should draw upon ourselves their holy and awesome <span class="h">da'as</span> until we are aroused on our own to draw close to Him — since they already revealed that Hashem is still with us. Therefore <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> was difficult all night — until Hashem sought and found in them some arousal from below. Then specifically the merit of the <span class="h">Avos</span> was aroused in completeness, the sea split, and they crossed in peace. For the full merit of the <span class="h">Avos</span> cannot be drawn — to complete the redemption — until we ourselves are aroused through the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> they drew upon us. Then specifically their great merit is drawn upon us, until we merit the complete redemption — as at <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span>, which was the completion of the redemption of Egypt.</div>

<div class="p">But now, an even more complete redemption is needed — a redemption after which there will be no exile at all — and therefore this exile is prolonged so greatly. Yet in the end, the redemption will be through the merit of the <span class="h">Avos</span> and as a free gift for His sake, as it is written: <span class="v">"And even this too, when they are in the land of their enemies…"</span> <span class="sr">(Leviticus 26:44)</span>, <span class="v">"And I shall remember My covenant with Yaakov…"</span> <span class="sr">(Leviticus 26:42)</span>, and <span class="v">"For My sake, for My sake, I shall do it"</span> <span class="sr">(Isaiah 48:11)</span>. But Hashem wants that at least we should arouse ourselves a little — each person from wherever he is — to draw close to Him. And we already have great power for this through the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> received from our fathers and our <span class="h">Rebbeim</span> and all the holy <span class="h">tzadikim</span> in every generation until now. Through this, we will merit the complete redemption speedily in our days, <span class="h">Amein</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 42 — The Song at the Sea: Yearning for the <i>Bais HaMikdash</i></div>

<div class="p">And this is what <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> wrote on the verse <span class="v">"You redeemed the tribe of Your inheritance, the mountain…"</span> — that immediately in the Song at the Sea they said: <span class="v">"You shall bring them and plant them on the mountain of Your inheritance"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 15:17)</span>. For there, in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, is the essential inclusiveness — the essential completeness. The entire <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> — where they sang the Song and concluded with the hope that the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> would be built, where the essential inclusiveness resides — this is the essential redemption, completed at <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 43 — Moshe's Staff: The Serpent Transformed</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, at the first revelation to Moshe at the <span class="h">sneh</span> [burning bush], [Hashem] told him to cast down the staff, as it is written: <span class="v">"Cast it to the ground, and it became a serpent"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 4:3)</span>. For Moshe was reluctant about the mission, saying he did not have the power to redeem them and illuminate in them the <span class="h">da'as</span> — because he knew there were people among Israel who had greatly corrupted [themselves]. Therefore Hashem told him to cast the staff to the ground. It is brought that Moshe's staff is the aspect of <span class="h">Metat</span>—, from which is the root of <span class="kc">free choice</span> — <span class="h">mateh</span> meaning to incline, that a person can incline either way.</div>

<div class="p">Hashem showed Moshe through this sign: he should understand the great power of free choice — and therefore a person is in awesome danger at every moment. He told him to cast <span class="kc">his</span> staff to the ground — for in truth, Moshe had already turned his free choice to good, having withstood every test. But [Hashem] showed him the power of choice of the masses and commanded him to cast the staff — the power of choice — to the ground: to cast it into the earthliness and materiality of the rest of the masses. Immediately when he cast it to the ground, <span class="kc">it became a serpent</span> — for among the majority of the world, lying in earthliness, the staff — the power of choice — turns into a serpent, due to the intensity of the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span>, the primordial serpent.</div>

<div class="p">Immediately He told him: <span class="v">"Stretch out your hand and grasp its tail… and it became a staff in his hand"</span> <span class="sr">(Exodus 4:4)</span> — to show him that <span class="kc">the measure of good is greater</span>, and he has the power to turn the staff from serpent back to staff. He has the power to help even those for whom choice turned into a serpent — to find merit in them, illuminate the holy <span class="h">da'as</span> that Hashem is still with us — until they are transformed from serpent to staff. Through this staff, all the signs and wonders were subsequently performed. For the essential redemption is through this: the <span class="h">tzadik</span> awakens the deeply fallen until he draws them close to Hashem. Therefore, at the completion of the redemption — <span class="h">Krias Yam Suf</span> — the redemption was specifically through the <span class="h">yichud</span> made through the aspect of the serpent that was transformed to holiness.</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§32–35</strong>: The progression from barley (<em>Omer</em>) to wheat (two loaves of <em>Shavuos</em>) = animal food to human food = rectifying Adam's sin. Even when eating is still "animal food," draw <em>yirah</em> and <em>Ratzon</em> there. Adam <em>trembled</em> at "you shall eat grass" — that trembling itself (= <em>yirah</em>) earned him the promise of bread (= human food). Exertions in Torah and drawing close to the <em>tzadik</em> = "the sweat of your brow."</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§36</strong>: The 320 (<em>sha"ch</em>) sparks refined each week: 7 × 45 (<em>Adam</em>) = 315, plus the <em>Hei</em> of the Name. Transforming <em>shatu</em> (folly) into <em>Adam</em> (man/da'as/Ratzon). The <em>remazim</em> are like light flickering through holes in partition walls — flashing into the sparks hidden in the <em>ruach shtus</em>, awakening them. <strong>Critical practical lesson: never despair.</strong></p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§37</strong>: Even literal madmen retain sparks of <em>da'as</em>. <em>Rabbainu</em>'s words: "If you want, you are not insane." Every sinner has the choice to overcome the <em>ruach shtus</em> through the <em>tzadik</em>'s <em>remazim</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§38</strong>: The <em>makifin</em> are drawn through speech — declaring <em>emunah</em> aloud. Daily prayer (<em>Pesukei D'Zimra</em>, <em>Shema</em>) = counting days = including day/night, sun/moon, <em>Ayeh</em>/<em>Melo</em>. <em>Kedushah</em> before <em>Shema</em> = "the whole earth is full / blessed from His place" = lower and upper.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§39–40 (<em>Krias Yam Suf</em>)</strong>: The sea = <em>yam hachochmah</em>: impossible to enter without a true <em>Rebbe</em> who knows the pathways. The difficulty: needing <em>is'arusa d'l'sata</em>. Israel achieved silence — "Hashem shall fight for you and you shall be silent" — and the sea split. Pharaoh (= external wisdom without silence) drowned. The hidden secret: the <em>yichud</em> was made through the serpent's bite = through the most fallen souls being raised by the <em>tzadik</em> through <em>Oraisa d'Atika Stima'ah</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§41</strong>: The merit of the <em>Avos</em> is activated only when we ourselves arouse — through the <em>da'as</em> they planted in us. The ultimate redemption will come the same way, speedily, <em>Amein</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§42–43</strong>: The Song at the Sea immediately yearns for the <em>Bais HaMikdash</em> = the essential inclusiveness. Moshe's staff becoming a serpent = free choice in the hands of the fallen masses; grasping the tail = the <em>tzadik</em>'s power to transform the serpent back to a staff. The redemption came specifically through the serpent transformed to holiness.</p>
